explanation:
```jsx
function Greeting({ name }) {
  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;
}

// used like an HTML tag:
<Greeting name="Ada" />
```
Components let you split the UI into independent pieces and think about each in isolation. The capital letter is how JSX tells your component apart from built-in tags like `<h1>`.

```jsx
const el = <h1 className="title">Hello, {user.name}</h1>;
// compiles to roughly:
const el2 = jsx('h1', { className: 'title', children: ['Hello, ', user.name] });
```
Curly braces `{}` embed any JavaScript expression. Because it's JS, attributes use camelCase (`className`, `onClick`) and every element must be closed (`<img />`).

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>;
}
```
Calling `setCount` does two things: stores the new value and schedules a re-render. Mutating a variable directly (`count++`) changes nothing on screen — only the setter triggers React.

```jsx
function Form() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');     // state
  const inputRef = useRef(null);            // DOM handle
  useEffect(() => inputRef.current.focus(), []); // side effect
  return <input ref={inputRef} value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value)} />;
}
```
The rules exist because React matches hook calls to their stored values by call order — a conditional call would shift every hook after it.

```jsx
function Card({ title, children }) {
  return (
    <section className="card">
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      {children}
    </section>
  );
}

<Card title="Profile">
  <Avatar user={user} />
  <Bio user={user} />
</Card>
```
React's docs are explicit: use composition, not inheritance — there is no case where a component class hierarchy is recommended.

```jsx
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';

const root = createRoot(document.getElementById('root'));
root.render(<App />);
```
react-dom also provides `createPortal` (render children into a different DOM node, e.g. modals) and `react-dom/server` for server-side rendering.

```jsx
// each render produces a cheap description, not real DOM nodes:
{ type: 'button', props: { className: 'primary', children: 'Save' } }
```
Diffing is heuristic: different element types rebuild the subtree, and list items are matched by `key` — which is why keys must be stable identifiers, not array indexes.

```jsx
const ThemeContext = createContext('light');

<ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
  <Page />
</ThemeContext.Provider>

// any depth below:
const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
```
Every consumer re-renders when the provided value changes, so context suits low-frequency global data (theme, locale, current user) — not rapidly-changing state.

```jsx
const Row = memo(function Row({ item, onSelect }) { /* … */ });

function List({ items }) {
  const sorted = useMemo(() => [...items].sort(byName), [items]);
  const onSelect = useCallback(id => setSelected(id), []);
  return sorted.map(item => <Row key={item.id} item={item} onSelect={onSelect} />);
}
```
Measure first with React DevTools Profiler — memoizing everything adds complexity for no gain when renders are already cheap.

```jsx
// custom hook: reusable logic, no wrapper components
function useWindowWidth() {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);
  useEffect(() => {
    const onResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);
    window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);
  }, []);
  return width;
}
```
HOCs (`withRouter(Comp)`) and render props (`<Mouse render={pos => …} />`) still appear in older codebases and interview questions, but hooks avoid their "wrapper hell".

```tsx
// TypeScript: checked at compile time
type Props = { name: string; age?: number };
function Profile({ name, age = 0 }: Props) {
  return <p>{name} ({age})</p>;
}

<Profile name="Ada" />        // ok
// <Profile name={42} />      // compile error
```
TypeScript catches mistakes before the code runs and covers everything (state, handlers, hooks), which is why PropTypes is rarely used in new projects.

```js
import { createSlice, configureStore } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

const counter = createSlice({
  name: 'counter',
  initialState: { value: 0 },
  reducers: { increment: (state) => { state.value += 1; } },
});
const store = configureStore({ reducer: { counter: counter.reducer } });
store.dispatch(counter.actions.increment());
```
Redux Toolkit (`createSlice`, `configureStore`) is the official way to write Redux today — it generates action creators and lets you write "mutating" logic safely via Immer.

```jsx
<Provider store={store}>
  <App />
</Provider>

function Counter() {
  const value = useSelector(state => state.counter.value);
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  return <button onClick={() => dispatch(increment())}>{value}</button>;
}
```
`useSelector` subscribes the component to the store and compares selector results by reference — select the smallest slice you need, not the whole state.

```js
const fetchUser = createAsyncThunk('user/fetch', async (id) => {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
  return res.json();
});

// in the slice:
extraReducers: (builder) => {
  builder
    .addCase(fetchUser.pending, (s) => { s.status = 'loading'; })
    .addCase(fetchUser.fulfilled, (s, a) => { s.status = 'idle'; s.user = a.payload; })
    .addCase(fetchUser.rejected, (s) => { s.status = 'error'; });
}
```
Alternatives: RTK Query (built-in data fetching/caching layer) and redux-saga in older codebases.

```jsx
test('increments on click', async () => {
  render(<Counter />);
  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Count: 0' }));
  expect(screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Count: 1' })).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```
The guiding principle: test behavior visible to users, not component internals — tests then survive refactors.

