```
`npm run dev` serves source over native ES modules (near-instant startup); `npm run build` produces the optimized `dist/` folder you deploy. Older equivalent: webpack + Babel (Create React App).

```jsx
// server (per request):
const html = renderToString(<App />);

// client entry — hydrate instead of createRoot().render():
hydrateRoot(document.getElementById('root'), <App />);
```
Hydration requires server and client markup to match, which is why frameworks like Next.js manage SSR for you (data fetching, streaming, routing included).

```jsx
// at build time, for each page:
const html = renderToString(<PostPage post={post} />);
writeFileSync(`dist/posts/${post.slug}.html`, wrapInDocument(html));
```
In Next.js this is the default for pages without dynamic data (with `generateStaticParams` for dynamic routes); ISR (incremental static regeneration) re-builds individual pages on a schedule to keep static content fresh.
